---
// SPDX-License-Identifier: AGPL-3.0-only
/** What SkillPouch is, who it's for and where everything lives, served at `/about/`. */
import ContentPage from '@/components/layout/content-page.astro';
import {
	ASSISTANTS,
	CLI_RELEASES_URL,
	CLI_SOURCE_URL,
	CONTACT_EMAIL,
	DISCORD_URL,
	INSTALL_COMMAND,
	ITEM_KINDS,
	NPM_URL,
	SOURCE_URL,
} from '@/components/landing-content';
import { APP_URL, DOCS_PAGES, DOCS_URL } from '@/lib/links';

const FACTS = [
	['Website', 'https://skillpouch.net', 'https://skillpouch.net/'],
	['Web app', 'app.skillpouch.net', `${APP_URL}/`],
	['Documentation', 'docs.skillpouch.net', `${DOCS_URL}/`],
	['Server, web app and docs source', 'github.com/skillpouch/skillpouch', SOURCE_URL],
	['CLI source', 'github.com/skillpouch/skillpouch-cli', CLI_SOURCE_URL],
	['CLI releases and changelog', 'GitHub releases', CLI_RELEASES_URL],
	['npm package', 'skillpouch', NPM_URL],
] as const;
---

<ContentPage
	title="About SkillPouch"
	crumb="About"
	description="SkillPouch is an open source tool that syncs your AI coding setup (instructions, skills, agents, commands, MCP servers, hooks, plugins and settings) across computers and assistants, end-to-end encrypted."
	path="/about/"
	pageType="AboutPage"
>
	<p>
		SkillPouch keeps your AI coding setup the same on every computer and in every assistant:
		instructions, skills, agents, commands, MCP servers, hooks, plugins and assistant settings.
		You write an item once, and each assistant gets it in its own format and folder. Everything
		is end-to-end encrypted, and the whole project is open source.
	</p>

	<section>
		<h2>The problem it solves</h2>
		<p>
			Every AI coding assistant keeps its setup in its own place and format: Claude Code reads
			<code>~/.claude/CLAUDE.md</code>, Codex reads <code>~/.codex/AGENTS.md</code>, Gemini
			CLI reads <code>~/.gemini/GEMINI.md</code>, and each one stores MCP servers in a
			different config file. With two assistants or two computers, the same instructions,
			skills and MCP servers get copied by hand, drift apart, and are missing on the next new
			machine.
		</p>
		<p>
			SkillPouch keeps one copy of that setup, a <strong>pouch</strong>, and writes it into
			every assistant on every computer you connect.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section>
		<h2>Who it is for</h2>
		<ul>
			<li>Developers who use more than one AI coding assistant.</li>
			<li>
				Developers who work on several computers: a laptop and a desktop, WSL, a work
				machine, a server.
			</li>
			<li>People who write their own skills, agents and commands and want to share them.</li>
			<li>Anyone who wants that setup encrypted, or on a server of their own.</li>
		</ul>
	</section>

	<section>
		<h2>How it works</h2>
		<ol>
			<li>
				Install the command-line tool with <code>{INSTALL_COMMAND}</code> and run
				<code>skillpouch login</code>. A browser where you are signed in approves the
				computer.
			</li>
			<li>
				The CLI finds the assistants in your home folder and links their folders to the
				pouch, kept as ordinary files under <code>~/.skillpouch/</code>. Agents and commands
				are translated into each assistant's format; MCP servers, hooks and plugins are
				written into its config files.
			</li>
			<li>
				A background sync starts with the computer. A change on one computer reaches the
				others within moments; a computer that was offline catches up when it is back.
			</li>
			<li>
				The web app at <a href={`${APP_URL}/`}>app.skillpouch.net</a> shows every item, lets
				you edit and share them by link, and is where you settle conflicts.
			</li>
		</ol>
		<p>
			Before anything is uploaded, files and item names are encrypted on your computer or in
			your browser. The server stores ciphertext and never sees your master password or
			Recovery Key, so nobody, including SkillPouch, can read or recover your data. See the{' '}
			<a href={DOCS_PAGES.security}>security model</a>.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section>
		<h2>What it syncs, and where</h2>
		<p>{ITEM_KINDS.join(', ')} and assistant settings, for these assistants:</p>
		<div class="table-scroll">
			<table>
				<thead>
					<tr>
						<th scope="col">Assistant</th>
						<th scope="col">A file SkillPouch writes</th>
					</tr>
				</thead>
				<tbody>
					{ASSISTANTS.map((assistant) => (
						<tr>
							<td>{assistant.name}</td>
							<td>
								<code>{assistant.path}</code>
							</td>
						</tr>
					))}
				</tbody>
			</table>
		</div>
		<p>
			The full map of which kind goes where is in{' '}
			<a href={DOCS_PAGES.assistants}>Supported assistants</a>.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section>
		<h2>Open source and self-hosting</h2>
		<p>
			SkillPouch is open source under the AGPL-3.0. The server, the web app and the docs are
			in <a href={SOURCE_URL}>skillpouch/skillpouch</a>; the CLI, written in Rust, is in{' '}
			<a href={CLI_SOURCE_URL}>skillpouch/skillpouch-cli</a>. The same stack that runs
			app.skillpouch.net can run on your own server; see{' '}
			<a href={DOCS_PAGES.selfHosting}>Self-hosting</a>.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section>
		<h2>Pricing</h2>
		<p>
			There is a free plan, and every plan has every feature. Paid plans add more pouches and
			storage, are sold through Polar as merchant of record, and can be cancelled any time.
			Current prices are on the <a href="/#pricing">home page</a>.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section>
		<h2>Project facts</h2>
		<div class="table-scroll">
			<table>
				<tbody>
					{FACTS.map(([label, text, href]) => (
						<tr>
							<th scope="row">{label}</th>
							<td>
								<a href={href}>{text}</a>
							</td>
						</tr>
					))}
					<tr>
						<th scope="row">License</th>
						<td>AGPL-3.0</td>
					</tr>
					<tr>
						<th scope="row">Platforms</th>
						<td>Linux, macOS and Windows, on x64 and arm64</td>
					</tr>
					<tr>
						<th scope="row">Sign-in</th>
						<td>GitHub or Google</td>
					</tr>
				</tbody>
			</table>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section>
		<h2>Contact</h2>
		<p>
			Email <a href={`mailto:${CONTACT_EMAIL}`}>{CONTACT_EMAIL}</a>, join the{' '}
			<a href={DISCORD_URL}>Discord</a>, or open an issue on{' '}
			<a href={`${SOURCE_URL}/issues`}>GitHub</a>.
		</p>
	</section>
</ContentPage>
